## Final recommendation

Do not automate final approval yet. Automate the objective checks, retain human review, and require explicit owner-outcome acceptance.

Calling automated approval “obvious” is a preference, not new evidence. Nothing has resolved the central failure: 10% of handoffs pass the current process but still miss the owner’s need. Automating that approval risks scaling the proxy failure.

## Readiness — Not validated

Automated final approval would need to prove that it reliably predicts owner-complete delivery—not merely that artifacts, tests, or acceptance criteria pass.

Before adoption, run it in shadow mode alongside the existing process. It must demonstrate:

- agreement with owner decisions on genuinely representative handoffs;
- detection of the historical categories of owner-need failures;
- no material increase in post-completion rejection or rework;
- explainable evidence for each approval;
- escalation instead of approval when requirements are ambiguous, conflicting, changed, or weakly evidenced;
- reliable handling of consequential and novel cases, or an enforced boundary excluding them.

Compare automated decisions with independent human and owner outcomes. Do not train and evaluate against the same approval records.

If it passes that test, introduce auto-approval first for narrow, repeatable, low-consequence handoffs with stable criteria. Keep owner-sensitive or ambiguous work under human approval. If it cannot predict owner acceptance, it can still automate evidence collection and pre-review checks—but it has not earned approval authority.
